      {
        "id": "p13-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $x$ if $2,x,32$ are in geometric progression.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\pm8$.",
          "worked": "The ratios of consecutive terms are equal.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x}{2}&=\\frac{32}{x}\\\\x^2&=64\\\\x&=\\pm8.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values work: the common ratio is $4$ or $-4$, respectively.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the values of $x$ and $y$ if $2,x,y,3$ are in geometric progression.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\sqrt[3]{12},\\quad y=\\sqrt[3]{18}$.",
          "worked": "Let the common ratio be $r$. Using $a_n=ar^{n-1}$,\n$$\\begin{align*}3&=2r^3\\\\r^3&=\\frac{3}{2}\\\\r&=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe intermediate terms are\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=2\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\x&=\\sqrt[3]{12}\\\\y&=2\\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)^2\\\\y&=\\sqrt[3]{18}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      }
    ]
  },
